  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nEastern Idaho remains positioned along the eastern periphery of\na broad, persistent upper-level low centered over western \nWashington. Southwest-to-westerly cyclonic flow around this \nsystem will continue streaming waves of shortwave energy over \nthe Central Mountains, maintaining shower and thunderstorm \nchances over the next 36 hours. Outside of the central mountain \nterrain, dry conditions and variable mid-to-high level cloud \ncover will prevail. Daytime maximum temperatures will climb back\nto near 80 degrees across the lower Snake Plain and areas \ntoward the Utah border today, expanding into widespread 80s \nacross the Snake Plain and Magic Valley on Saturday. Mountain \nprecipitation will briefly wane Saturday afternoon as the parent\nlow begins a slow eastward translation across the Pacific \nNorthwest.\n\nTemperatures will continue their upward trend on Sunday as \nsoutherly flow strengthens ahead of the approaching trough, \nboosting valley highs into the 80s amidst breezy afternoon \nwinds. Increased cloud shading and widespread precipitation will\nhold off through most of the day, preserving favorable outdoor \nconditions until late Sunday evening. Moisture and dynamic lift \nincrease sharply Sunday night through Labor Day Monday as the \ntrough axis crosses Idaho. Operational models and ensemble \nguidance display ongoing spread regarding QPF totals: the ECMWF \nmaintains a wetter solution compared to the drier GFS. A middle-\nof-the-road consensus blend remains in place, forecasting \nroughly 0.25\" to 0.50\" of wetting rain across much of eastern \nIdaho, though these totals remain subject to adjustment as \nguidance converges. Extensive cloud cover and rain will cap \nMonday highs in the 60s to lower 70s. As the core low lifts into\nAlberta late Monday afternoon, precipitation will diminish from\nwest to east alongside clearing skies.\n\nConditions dry out rapidly on Tuesday as temperatures begin a\nfast recovery under rising 500 mb heights. Global ensembles show\nhigh-pressure ridging attempting to rebuild across the\nSouthwestern U.S. mid-to-late week, positioning eastern Idaho\nalong the northern margin of the ridge axis. Model guidance\ndisplays noticeable spread regarding how aggressively the ridge\namplifies, but lower elevations are forecast to return to the\nlower 80s as early as Wednesday, with potential to trend even\nwarmer if the ridge axis shifts further north. Southwest flow\naloft along the ridge periphery will keep low-end, daily\nprecipitation chances in the forecast for higher terrain zones\nthrough late week.",
